Keyword lines among lines that do not parse, with no `;` written anywhere
(issues #351, #355). `:IF bReady` parses, so it is terminated. The raw
`delete` lines are not, and neither is `:ELSE`, because the raw line after
it fails too: terminating `:ELSE` would make that line a second failing
statement. `:ENDIF` has only parsing code after it, so it is decided on
its own and terminated. The run from the first `delete` to `:ENDIF;`
stays one statement and one unexpected_token, and the keyword lines keep
their block indentation.
-- input.ssl --
:DECLARE bReady;
:IF bReady
  delete from STAGING
:ELSE
  delete from ARCHIVE
:ENDIF
-- format --
:DECLARE bReady;

:IF bReady;
  delete from STAGING
:ELSE
  delete from ARCHIVE
:ENDIF;
-- diagnostics --
3:3-3:9 error unexpected_token: Unexpected identifier 'delete' - expected an operator or ';' (is the previous statement missing its ';'?)
-- diagnostics [--strict --info] --
3:3-3:9 warning undeclared_variable: Variable 'delete' is not declared
3:3-3:9 error unexpected_token: Unexpected identifier 'delete' - expected an operator or ';' (is the previous statement missing its ';'?)
3:10-3:14 warning undeclared_variable: Variable 'from' is not declared
3:15-3:22 warning undeclared_variable: Variable 'STAGING' is not declared
5:15-5:22 warning undeclared_variable: Variable 'ARCHIVE' is not declared
